\title{Retrocausal Proof Theory:\\Exact Limits of Confirmation by Consequences}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a propositional model of retrocausal proof theory, in which a candidate proposition is assessed through consequences that have been independently verified and found mutually coherent. The central result gives an exact boundary: for a fixed proposition $P$, the rule that infers $P$ from every verified consequence $Q$ satisfying $P\Rightarrow Q$ is valid if and only if $P$ is already true. An unrestricted version therefore yields every proposition, including falsehood. The singleton family containing the always-true proposition supplies a universal control: it is a verified, coherent consequence of every candidate, including falsehood, but cannot recover a false antecedent. We characterize the additional information that makes backward reasoning sound. A backward certificate is an implication from the joint truth of the verified consequences to the candidate. Such a certificate suffices for recovery; for a single consequence, two-way certification is exactly logical equivalence. Recovery also follows when one listed consequence is backward-complete, and persists when further verified consequences are appended. These results separate proof validity from proof search. Consequences may guide search, but validity and compression claims must account for the backward certificate.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Ordinary deduction moves from a proposition to its consequences. Given $P$ and $P\Rightarrow Q$, one concludes $Q$. Retrocausal proof theory asks whether useful traffic can move in the other direction. If a candidate predicts $Q_1,\ldots,Q_n$, and these consequences have all been verified, can their success establish the candidate?

The question resembles scientific confirmation and software testing. A theory gains credibility when predictions are observed, and a candidate program becomes plausible when it passes tests. Deductive validity is stricter: distinct antecedents can share consequences, so $P\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q$ do not generally entail $P$.

This paper locates the exact boundary and gives a sound positive replacement. The obstruction is already propositional and therefore precedes questions about arithmetic syntax, proof calculi, or computational complexity. A fixed proposition admits uniform confirmation from arbitrary verified consequences exactly when it is already true. A universal rule collapses the logic. Verification entails coherence, but coherence does not identify an antecedent. Sound reversal is restored by an explicit reconstruction implication from the verified bundle to the target.

The result does not make consequence-guided reasoning useless. It distinguishes two roles. Consequences may rank or prune candidate derivations. Acceptance of the target must still rest on a derivation or a backward certificate. This distinction is especially important for claims of proof compression, where the certificate's size must be counted.

\section{Propositional framework}
Let $P$ be a candidate proposition and let $\mathcal Q=[Q_1,\ldots,Q_n]$ be a finite list.

\begin{definition}[Family of consequences]
The list $\mathcal Q$ is a family of consequences of $P$ if $P\Rightarrow Q_i$ for every $i$.
\end{definition}

This is a predictive condition. It asserts neither $P$ nor any $Q_i$.

\begin{definition}[Joint verification]
The list $\mathcal Q$ is jointly verified if every member is true, equivalently if $Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n$ holds. The empty conjunction is $\top$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coherence]
The list $\mathcal Q$ is coherent if its joint truth does not imply falsehood:
\[
\neg\bigl((Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Rightarrow\bot\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Backward certificate]
A backward certificate for $P$ relative to $\mathcal Q$ is an implication
\[
(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Rightarrow P.
\]
It is a reconstruction map from the verified bundle to the candidate.
\end{definition}

These notions distinguish prediction, observed truth, compatibility, and recoverability.

\section{The exact boundary}
\begin{theorem}[Uniform Confirmation Boundary]
For every proposition $P$,
\[
\bigl[\forall Q,\,((P\Rightarrow Q)\Rightarrow Q\Rightarrow P)\bigr]
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad P.
\]
Equivalently, $P$ can be inferred uniformly from every true consequence precisely when $P$ is already true.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assume the uniform property and choose $Q=\top$. The implication $P\Rightarrow\top$ holds and $\top$ is true, so the property yields $P$. Conversely, if $P$ holds, then for arbitrary $Q$ the desired conclusion $P$ is already available, irrespective of the two premises.
\end{proof}

The characterization shows that uniform consequence-confirmation adds no new source of validity.

\begin{corollary}[Universal collapse]
If
\[
\forall P\,\forall Q,\quad (P\Rightarrow Q)\Rightarrow Q\Rightarrow P,
\]
then every proposition is true.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Fix arbitrary $P$ and instantiate the rule with $Q=\top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No unrestricted retrocausal rule]
There is no sound rule satisfying
\[
\forall P\,\forall Q,\quad (P\Rightarrow Q)\Rightarrow Q\Rightarrow P.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Set $P=\bot$ and $Q=\top$. Both premises hold, while the conclusion is false.
\end{proof}

The always-true proposition is maximally nondiscriminating. Because it follows from every antecedent, verifying it carries no information that selects an antecedent.

\section{Verification, coherence, and the universal control}
\begin{proposition}[Verified consequences are coherent]
Every jointly verified finite list of propositions is coherent.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $V=Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n$ be the verified conjunction. If $V\Rightarrow\bot$, applying that implication to the available proof of $V$ yields contradiction. Hence the list is coherent.
\end{proof}

This is only a compatibility result and supplies no backward identification.

\begin{theorem}[Always-true control]
For every candidate $P$, the singleton list $[\top]$ is a family of consequences of $P$, is jointly verified, and is coherent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every proposition implies $\top$; $\top$ is true; and its truth does not imply $\bot$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[False antecedent counterexample]
For $P=\bot$, the list $[\top]$ is a verified, coherent family of consequences but possesses no backward certificate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The forward implication $\bot\Rightarrow\top$ is vacuous, and $\top$ is verified and coherent. A backward certificate would be $\top\Rightarrow\bot$, which is false.
\end{proof}

Thus all advertised forward checks can pass while recovery fails. More nondiscriminating consequences do not help: the issue is whether their conjunction excludes worlds in which $P$ is false.

\section{Sound backward reconstruction}
\begin{theorem}[Backward Recovery]
Let $P$ be a proposition and $\mathcal Q=[Q_1,\ldots,Q_n]$. Suppose $P\Rightarrow Q_i$ for every $i$, every $Q_i$ is true, and
\[
(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Rightarrow P.
\]
Then $P$ is true.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Joint verification supplies the conjunction. Applying the backward certificate yields $P$.
\end{proof}

The forward conditions justify the word ``consequence,'' while verification and the certificate perform the recovery.

\begin{theorem}[Singleton two-way certification]
If $P\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q\Rightarrow P$, then $P\Longleftrightarrow Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The assumptions are exactly the two directions of logical equivalence.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One complete consequence suffices]
Let $\mathcal Q$ be jointly verified. If $R$ occurs in $\mathcal Q$ and $R\Rightarrow P$, then $P$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Joint verification gives $R$, and the implication gives $P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity under verified extension]
Let $\mathcal B$ be a base list and $\mathcal E$ an extension. If the joint truth of $\mathcal B$ implies $P$, and every member of the concatenation $\mathcal B+\mathcal E$ is verified, then $P$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Verification of the concatenation includes verification of the base. Apply the base certificate.
\end{proof}

Additional verified evidence therefore does not destroy an existing reconstruction, although arbitrary extension cannot create one automatically.

\section{Finite semantic interpretation}
Let $\Omega$ be a finite set of possible worlds and represent each proposition by its truth set. Then $P\Rightarrow Q$ means $[P]\subseteq[Q]$, while the joint truth set of the family is
\[
\bigcap_{i=1}^n[Q_i].
\]
A backward certificate requires
\[
\bigcap_{i=1}^n[Q_i]\subseteq[P].
\]
Combined with forward consequencehood, this gives equality:
\[
[P]=\bigcap_{i=1}^n[Q_i].
\]

Coherence asks only that the intersection be nonempty. Recovery asks that every world in the intersection satisfy $P$. This explains the gap: a nonempty compatible region may contain worlds outside $[P]$. The control $\top$ corresponds to all of $\Omega$ and narrows nothing.

This also gives an information-theoretic reading. Evidence shared by many incompatible antecedents contains insufficient identifying information. A backward certificate ensures that no world compatible with the evidence lies outside the target.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
\subsection{Exhaustive boundary audit}
On a finite world set, encode $P$ and each $Q_i$ as Boolean truth vectors. Scan all worlds to check forward implication and backward implication. At an observed world, separately record which propositions are verified. For $m$ worlds and $k$ consequences, the scan costs $O(mk)$ time and constant auxiliary space beyond the input. Inserting the all-true vector demonstrates that forward implication plus observed truth need not provide recovery.

\subsection{Backward-certificate checker}
Compute the pointwise conjunction $C=Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_k$. The certificate succeeds exactly when no world satisfies $C\land\neg P$. The procedure costs $O(mk)$ time and can return a counterexample world explaining failure. If all listed propositions are observed true and the certificate succeeds, the Backward Recovery Theorem yields the target.

\subsection{Consequence-guided search}
For a fixed proof calculus, maintain a frontier of candidate proof states. Predict consequences for each state and score the state by the number or weight of already verified predictions. Expand higher-scoring states first. Accept the target only when an ordinary derivation or explicit backward certificate is produced. If $N$ states are explored and each is compared with $k$ consequences, scoring adds $O(Nk)$ work to the calculus-specific expansion cost. The heuristic may reduce $N$ empirically but does not alter validity.

\subsection{Compression accounting}
Fix an encoding and let $L(P)$ be the shortest direct proof length. For consequence proofs $\pi_i$, a backward certificate $\beta$, and reconstruction overhead $r$, define
\[
L_{\mathrm{route}}=\sum_i|\pi_i|+|\beta|+r.
\]
A genuine compression requires $L_{\mathrm{route}}<L(P)$. Omitting $|\beta|$ can hide the original proof in the reconstruction mechanism. Constant-factor claims therefore require a fixed calculus, target class, encoding, and comparison baseline.

\section{Applications}
\paragraph{Arithmetic search.}
Parity, congruence, inequality, and small-instance consequences can reject candidate arithmetic derivations or rank surviving paths. Their success does not prove a universal statement; final acceptance still requires a derivation or certified equivalence.

\paragraph{Program synthesis.}
Program candidates imply outputs on test inputs. Passing tests verifies consequences, but many incorrect programs can agree on a finite suite. A backward certificate is a completeness theorem showing that, within a specified program class, passing all tests entails the specification.

\paragraph{Diagnosis.}
A disease may imply symptoms, but observed coherent symptoms need not identify the disease. Recovery requires a discriminating condition excluding alternatives. This mirrors the distinction between predictive sensitivity and identifiability.

\paragraph{Scientific inference.}
Predictions may confirm a theory without deductively determining it. A uniqueness theorem, restricted model class, or reverse implication is required to turn successful prediction into logical recovery.

\paragraph{Invertible transformations.}
Definitional unfolding, equivalence-preserving normalization, invertible inference rules, and conservative translations are promising settings. They can expose easier consequences while carrying compositional reconstruction certificates.

\section{Discussion}
Any proposed retrocausal validity rule must survive the $\top$ control. Since every candidate implies $\top$, criteria based only on forward consequencehood, truth, and coherence accept evidence compatible with every antecedent. They cannot distinguish true candidates from false ones.

The positive theory assigns different tasks to consequences and certificates. Consequences organize exploration; certificates preserve validity. This resembles certified computation, where a flexible process discovers an answer and a compact checked object justifies it.

Certificate accounting is essential for compression. A consequence may be shorter precisely because it discards information. Recovery restores that information, and the certificate bears the cost. Compression is possible when a structured target admits economical consequences and a short inverse, not merely because many easy consequences exist.

Coherence remains a useful filter. Incoherent predictions refute a candidate immediately, but coherent predictions may leave many candidates. Finite experiments can quantify ambiguity by counting compatible hypotheses or measuring the reduction in logarithmic hypothesis count.

\section{Future work}
Promising directions include the following.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Separate proof-search semantics from proof-validity semantics, using consequences to rank candidates while requiring a standard derivation at acceptance.
\item Fix proof languages, derivation trees, and size measures for fragments of arithmetic so that compression claims become precise.
\item Measure certificate complexity together with the lengths of consequence proofs, preventing hidden reconstruction costs.
\item Study restricted classes generated by invertible rules, definitional unfolding, equivalences, and conservative transformations.
\item Compare ordinary and consequence-guided enumeration on finite arithmetic corpora, recording explored nodes and final proof lengths.
\item Replace propositional joint truth with syntactic consistency relative to a recursively presented theory, while retaining the warning that consistency does not identify an arbitrary sentence.
\item Develop information-theoretic lower bounds from finite hypothesis spaces in which many incompatible antecedents share consequence profiles.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
The unrestricted retrocausal idea meets an exact barrier. A proposition can be inferred uniformly from all of its verified consequences if and only if it is already true, and a universal rule would establish falsehood. Verification guarantees coherence but not identification; the always-true consequence is the universal counterexample.

The sound replacement is explicit backward certification. Jointly verified consequences recover a candidate when their conjunction implies it. For a singleton this is equivalence, one backward-complete family member can suffice, and recovery persists under verified extension.

Consequences can therefore illuminate search, expose failure, and prioritize candidates. Certificates cross the final logical bridge. Maintaining that division permits consequence-driven discovery without weakening deductive validity, and provides the correct accounting framework for future claims of proof compression.

\end{document}

